### A. The Mid-Season Expansion to Multi-Venue Simultaneous Scheduling

As the tournament progressed into the summer months, the committee confronted increasing pressure to distribute fixtures more broadly across the national territory, a demand driven both by provincial governmental actors and by the commercial interests of regional media enterprises seeking local broadcasting content. On 19 July 1992, the tournament committee scheduled three simultaneous matches across the country. This decision represented a significant departure from the sequential single-venue model that had characterized the early rounds of the tournament, and it raised novel questions regarding the committee's authority to impose simultaneous hosting obligations on multiple provincial administrative units.^{4} On 19 July 1992, the tournament committee scheduled three simultaneous matches across the country. The logistical complexity of coordinating concurrent events across different time zones—though China observes a single official time zone, practical operational differences between eastern coastal and interior regions remained relevant—was substantial, and some members of the committee's advisory board reportedly expressed reservations regarding the adequacy of existing supervisory mechanisms.^{5}

Match Alpha was designated for the capital, while Match Beta and Match Gamma were sent to regional venues. The allocation of Match Alpha to Beijing was consistent with established precedent and attracted relatively limited controversy; the capital's hosting infrastructure, including the Workers' Stadium complex and associated security apparatus, had been regularly utilized for high-profile national fixtures since the mid-1980s.^{6} The decision to assign the remaining two matches to provincial venues, however, generated considerable debate. Match Alpha was designated for the capital, while Match Beta and Match Gamma were sent to regional venues. Critics contended that the selection criteria for these regional sites lacked transparency and that the committee had failed to comply with the consultation procedures established under Article 37 of the revised Organic Law. Supporters of the committee's decision, by contrast, characterized the regional allocation as a laudable exercise of decentralizing discretion, consistent with the broader policy orientation of the State Council's 1990 directive on balanced territorial development, which had encouraged the distribution of nationally significant cultural and sporting events beyond the traditional axis of Beijing and Shanghai.^{7}

